{title2=Group Theory}

= 1
{parent=B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

For a <group action>, the <orbit> and <stabilizer> are
$$
Gx=\{gx:g\in G\},\qquad G_x=\{g\in G:gx=x\}.
$$
The <orbit-stabilizer theorem> for a <finite group> states
$$
\boxed{|G|=|Gx|\,|G_x|.}
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

For a <regular tetrahedron>, rotations fixing a vertex rotate about the line from that vertex through the opposite face centre. Their angles are $0,2\pi/3,4\pi/3$, so the <stabilizer> is a <cyclic group> of order three. Rotations act transitively on the four <vertices>, giving $|G_1|=4\cdot3=12$ by the <orbit-stabilizer theorem>.

For a <cube>, the corresponding axis is the body diagonal through the fixed vertex and its opposite. The three incident edges can be cyclically permuted by the same three angles, so again the <stabilizer> is cyclic of order three. The <orbit> has eight vertices. Hence
$$
\boxed{|G_1|=12,\qquad |G_2|=24.}
$$

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Realize the cube's vertices as $(\pm1,\pm1,\pm1)$ and take the four with coordinate product $+1$. They form a <regular tetrahedron>, and their negatives form the other one. Every proper rotation of the first tetrahedron also permutes the negatives, hence is a cube rotation. This embeds $G_1$ as a <subgroup> of $G_2$.

The embedded <group> preserves each of these two four-vertex sets and acts transitively on each. Thus the resulting action on all eight cube vertices has **two orbits, each of size four**.

