= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

A <prime number> is an integer $p>1$ whose only positive divisors are $1$ and $p$. The restriction $p>1$ excludes the unit $1$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The <fundamental theorem of arithmetic> says that every integer $n>1$ is a product of <prime numbers>, uniquely up to reordering. Equivalently, $n=\prod_p p^{a_p}$ for a unique family of nonnegative integer exponents, only finitely many of which are nonzero.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

If the complete list of <prime numbers> were $p_1,\ldots,p_k$, then $N=p_1\cdots p_k+1>1$ would have a prime divisor by the <fundamental theorem of arithmetic>. No $p_i$ divides it, since $N\equiv1\pmod{p_i}$. This contradicts completeness of the list. Hence **there are infinitely many prime numbers**.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Every integer has one of the six residues $0,1,2,3,4,5$ modulo $6$. A <prime number> other than $2$ or $3$ is divisible by neither $2$ nor $3$, eliminating residues $0,2,3,4$. Thus
$$
\boxed{p\equiv1\text{ or }-1\pmod6.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Suppose there were only finitely many <prime numbers> congruent to $5$ modulo $6$, and list them as $p_1,\ldots,p_k$. The list is nonempty since it contains $5$. Let $N=6p_1\cdots p_k-1$. It is greater than one and divisible by neither $2$ nor $3$. Every prime factor therefore has residue $1$ or $-1$ modulo $6$. Since $N\equiv-1\pmod6$, at least one prime factor $q$ must have residue $-1$. But $N\equiv-1\pmod{p_i}$ for every listed prime, so $q$ is not on the list. This contradiction proves **there are infinitely many primes congruent to $5$ modulo $6$**.

= D
