= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

A $k$-dimensional embedded <submanifold> of $\mathbb R^n$ is a subset $M$ such that near each $p\in M$ there is a smooth <diffeomorphism> of ambient open neighbourhoods that sends $M$ to the coordinate plane $\mathbb R^k\times\{0\}^{n-k}$. More precisely, for some open $U\ni p$ and diffeomorphism $\Phi:U\to V\subseteq\mathbb R^n$,
$$
\Phi(M\cap U)=V\cap(\mathbb R^k\times\{0\}^{n-k}).
$$
Thus locally the subset has $k$ freely varying smooth coordinates.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

A <tangent vector> to $M$ at $p$ is a velocity $\gamma'(0)$ of a smooth curve $\gamma:(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)\to M$ with $\gamma(0)=p$. The <tangent space> is the set of all such velocities:
$$
\boxed{T_pM=\{\gamma'(0):\gamma(0)=p,\ \gamma\text{ a smooth curve in }M\}.}
$$
It is a $k$-dimensional linear subspace of $\mathbb R^n$; for a local parametrization $\phi$, it is the image of the injective <derivative> $D\phi$ at the corresponding parameter point.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Let $g(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2-z^2$. The upper cone away from the apex is the part of $g^{-1}(0)$ with $z>0$. There
$$
\nabla g=(2x,2y,-2z)\ne0.
$$
The <regular level set theorem> says that a level set of a smooth scalar function with nonzero <gradient> is an embedded <submanifold> of codimension one. Intersecting it with the open half-space $z>0$ preserves this local property. Thus **$M$ is a two-dimensional submanifold of $\mathbb R^3$**.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The <tangent space> to a regular level set is the kernel of its <derivative>. Writing $z_0=\sqrt{x_0^2+y_0^2}$ gives
$$
\boxed{T_{v_0}M=\{(\xi,\eta,\zeta):x_0\xi+y_0\eta-z_0\zeta=0\}.}
$$
If an affine tangent plane through $v_0$ is intended, its equation is $x_0(x-x_0)+y_0(y-y_0)-z_0(z-z_0)=0$. Since $x_0^2+y_0^2=z_0^2$, this simplifies to the same plane $x_0x+y_0y-z_0z=0$, which passes through the apex.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Every point of the indicated ray has the form $w_0=(r,2r,\sqrt5\,r)$ with $r>0$; the apex is excluded. Substitution into the preceding <tangent space> formula gives
$$
\boxed{T_{w_0}M=\{(\xi,\eta,\zeta):\xi+2\eta-\sqrt5\,\zeta=0\},}
$$
independent of $r$. The affine tangent plane has the same equation, since the ray lies in this plane. Thus both interpretations of the requested tangent plane are constant along this generator of the <cone>.

= G
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Calculus of Variations}

= 1
{parent=G}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

Let $P=F_{y'}$ and $Q=F_{y''}$. For a smooth <variation> $y+\varepsilon\eta$, the <first variation> is
$$
\delta J=\int_a^b(F_y\eta+P\eta'+Q\eta'')\,dx
=\int_a^b(F_y-P'+Q'')\eta\,dx+[(P-Q')\eta+Q\eta']_a^b.
$$
Here the last expression follows by two integrations by parts. Taking variations supported in the interior and applying the <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> gives the <Euler-Lagrange equation for a second-derivative functional>:
$$
\boxed{F_y-\frac{d}{dx}F_{y'}+\frac{d^2}{dx^2}F_{y''}=0.}
$$
If both endpoint values and slopes are free, their independent variations force the <natural boundary conditions for second-derivative functionals>
$$
\boxed{Q=0,\qquad P-Q'=0\quad\text{at }a,b.}
$$
If an endpoint value or slope is fixed instead, its corresponding variation vanishes and the associated natural condition is not required.

For $H=y''Q-y'(Q'-P)-F$, differentiate using the <chain rule>:
$$
H'=y'(P'-Q''-F_y)-F_x.
$$
The <Euler-Lagrange equation> cancels the first term. Consequently $H'=-F_x$, and if there is no explicit $x$ dependence,
$$
\boxed{H=y''F_{y''}+y'\bigl(F_{y'}-(F_{y''})'\bigr)-F\text{ is constant}.}
$$
This is a <Beltrami identity for a second-derivative functional>.

= H
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Classical Mechanics}

= 1
{parent=H}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

The <inertia tensor> about the centroid has entries $J_{ij}=\int_B\rho(r^2\delta_{ij}-x_ix_j)\,dV$. Reflection symmetry makes every off-diagonal entry zero. Under $x=a\xi$, $y=a\eta$, $z=c\zeta$, the ellipsoid becomes the unit ball. Its three coordinate second moments are equal, and
$$
\int_{\text{ball}}\xi^2\,dV=\frac13\int_{\text{ball}}r^2\,dV=\frac{4\pi}{15}.
$$
Using the stated mass gives $\int_B\rho x^2\,dV=Ma^2/5$, the same for $y$, and $\int_B\rho z^2\,dV=Mc^2/5$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{J^{(G)}=\frac M5\operatorname{diag}(a^2+c^2,\ a^2+c^2,\ 2a^2).}
$$
For the parallel coordinate axes based at $P=(0,0,c)$, the <parallel axis theorem> in tensor form is $J^{(P)}=J^{(G)}+M(|d|^2I-dd^T)$, where $d=P-G$. Thus
$$
\boxed{J^{(P)}=\frac M5\operatorname{diag}(a^2+6c^2,\ a^2+6c^2,\ 2a^2).}
$$

= J
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Quantum Theory}

= 1
{parent=J}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

The one-dimensional <time-independent Schrödinger equation> is
$$
-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\psi''(x)+V(x)\psi(x)=E\psi(x).
$$
For the <infinite square well> on $[0,a]$, $V=0$ inside and the <wave function> is zero outside. The boundary conditions are $\psi(0)=\psi(a)=0$. For positive integer $n$, $\psi_n=A\sin(n\pi x/a)$ satisfies them, and $\psi_n''=-(n\pi/a)^2\psi_n$. Substitution gives the <energy levels>
$$
\boxed{E_n=\frac{n^2\pi^2\hbar^2}{2ma^2}.}
$$
Normalization requires $1=\int_0^a|\psi_n|^2dx=A^2a/2$. The requested real positive constant is therefore
$$
\boxed{A=\sqrt{\frac2a}.}
$$

= K
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics and Waves}

= 1
{parent=K}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

A <streamline> is a curve whose tangent is parallel to the instantaneous fluid <velocity field>. In steady flow one may parametrize it by $d\mathbf r/ds=\mathbf u(\mathbf r)$. Dotting the steady <Euler equations> with $\mathbf u$ eliminates the cross-product term, since $\mathbf u\cdot((\nabla\times\mathbf u)\times\mathbf u)=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbf u\cdot\nabla\left(p+\tfrac12\rho|\mathbf u|^2\right)=0,}
$$
which is <Bernoulli's principle> along each streamline.

For the supplied field, $xu+yv=0$, so along a streamline
$$
\frac{d}{ds}(x^2+y^2)=2xu+2yv=0.
$$
Writing $r^2=x^2+y^2$, the tangential speed is $v_\theta=-br+c/r$ and the radial speed is zero. Both $r$ and $|\mathbf u|^2=(-br+c/r)^2$ are constant around each nonstagnant circular streamline. The Bernoulli quantity is constant there, so **the pressure is constant on every such circle**.

If a circle is wholly stagnant, the same conclusion follows directly from the steady <Euler equations>: on it $\mathbf u=0$ and $\nabla|\mathbf u|^2=0$, hence $\nabla p=0$. Equivalently the radial momentum equation gives $\nabla p=\rho(-br+c/r)^2\mathbf e_r/r$ and no angular pressure variation, including at zero tangential speed. Thus **$p$ is constant on $x^2+y^2=a^2$ for every nonzero $a$**, with no exception for a stagnation circle.

= 2
{parent=K}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}
{c}

Use the convention that positive vortex strength is counterclockwise and complex velocity is $u-iv$. A <complex potential of a two-dimensional potential flow> for the superposed <uniform flow> and two <point vortices> is
$$
\boxed{W(z)=Uz+\frac{\Gamma}{2\pi i}\log(z-a)-\frac{\Gamma}{2\pi i}\log(z+a).}
$$
Local choices of logarithm branch change no velocity. Differentiation gives
$$
W'(z)=U-\frac{i\Gamma a}{\pi(z^2-a^2)}.
$$
For nonzero vortex strength and $U>0$, its two zeros are
$$
\boxed{z_\pm=\pm\sqrt{a^2+\frac{i\Gamma a}{\pi U}}.}
$$
The number under the square root is nonzero, so these are distinct <stagnation points>, neither at a vortex.

A free <point vortex> moves with the velocity induced by the other vortex and the background, omitting its own singular field. At either vortex, the other induces vertical velocity $-\Gamma/(4\pi a)$, while the background contributes $(U,0)$. Their velocities are therefore equal. Their separation stays $(2a,0)$, making this induced velocity constant for all time. Both travel along parallel straight lines with
$$
\boxed{\mathbf V=\left(U,-\frac{\Gamma}{4\pi a}\right),\qquad
|\mathbf V|=\sqrt{U^2+\frac{\Gamma^2}{16\pi^2a^2}}.}
$$

= M
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Probability}

= 1
{parent=M}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

A <Poisson distribution> with parameter $\lambda\geq0$ has
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X=k)=e^{-\lambda}\frac{\lambda^k}{k!},\quad k=0,1,2,\ldots;\qquad
\mathbb E[X]=\operatorname{Var}X=\lambda.}
$$
For $\lambda=0$ it is concentrated at zero. For $\lambda>0$, shifting the index in the defining series gives $\mathbb E[X]=\lambda$ and $\mathbb E[X(X-1)]=\lambda^2$, so $\operatorname{Var}X=\lambda^2+\lambda-\lambda^2=\lambda$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Independence and convolution give
$$
\mathbb P(X+Y=k)=e^{-(\mu+\lambda)}\sum_{j=0}^k\frac{\mu^j\lambda^{k-j}}{j!(k-j)!}
=e^{-(\mu+\lambda)}\frac{(\mu+\lambda)^k}{k!},
$$
by the <binomial theorem>. Thus
$$
\boxed{X+Y\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\mu+\lambda).}
$$

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

By the addition rule for independent <Poisson random variables>, $X_n$ has the same distribution as a sum of $n$ independent variables $Z_j\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda)$. Each has <expectation> and <variance> $\lambda$. The <central limit theorem> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{X_n-n\lambda}{\sqrt n}\ \xrightarrow{d}\ N(0,\lambda).}
$$
For $\lambda=0$, every variable is zero, and the conclusion is the degenerate normal law with zero variance.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

A rate-two <Poisson process> starts at $N_0=0$, has independent increments and stationary increments, and has $N_{t+h}-N_t\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(2h)$ for $h\geq0$ when time is measured in minutes. Equivalently it is a counting process with independent stationary increments satisfying, uniformly in the start time,
$$
\mathbb P(\Delta N=1)=2h+o(h),\qquad \mathbb P(\Delta N\geq2)=o(h)
$$
as $h\downarrow0$. Thus an average rate alone is insufficient; the independence and increment assumptions are part of the model.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The one-minute increment has <Poisson distribution> with parameter two, independently of where that interval occurs. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(N_{11:01}-N_{11:00}=0)=e^{-2}\approx0.1353.}
$$

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The full-day count has <Poisson distribution> with mean and variance $2\cdot450=900$. Its <normal approximation> is $N(900,900)$, with standard deviation $30$. With the usual <continuity correction>,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(N_{450}\geq930)\approx1-\Phi\left(\frac{929.5-900}{30}\right)\approx0.163.}
$$
Without the half-unit correction the central-limit approximation gives $1-\Phi(1)\approx0.159$.

= 2
{parent=M}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Condition on the intermediate state. Starting at $+1$, a return to $+1$ after two transitions occurs through $-1$ with probability $q(1-p)$ or through $+1$ with probability $(1-q)^2$. These alternatives are disjoint, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X_3=+1\mid X_1=+1)=q(1-p)+(1-q)^2.}
$$

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

A <stationary distribution> is a probability row vector $\pi$ satisfying $\pi P=\pi$. Its coordinates obey $\pi_-(1-p)=\pi_+q$ and $\pi_-+\pi_+=1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\pi_-=\frac q{1-p+q},\qquad\pi_+=\frac{1-p}{1-p+q}.}
$$
Because both transitions between distinct states have positive probability, the finite <Markov chain> is irreducible; its positive self-transition probabilities also make it aperiodic.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The finite irreducible aperiodic <Markov chain> converges to its <stationary distribution>, regardless of its initial law. Therefore $\mathbb E[X_j]\to\pi_+-\pi_-$. Taking the arithmetic means of this convergent sequence gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac1n\mathbb E[S_n]=\frac{1-p-q}{1-p+q}.}
$$
This uses <linearity of expectation> and convergence of Cesàro means; it does not require the variables $X_j$ to be independent.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

With probability $2/3$, the chain starts at $+1$ and $T=1$. Conditional on starting at $-1$, it takes a geometric number $G$ of transitions to first reach $+1$, with success probability $1-p$, so $T=1+G$. The <probability generating function> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[s^T]=\frac23s+\frac13s\frac{(1-p)s}{1-ps}
=\frac23s+\frac{(1-p)s^2}{3(1-ps)}.}
$$
The parameter $q$ is absent because the process stops at its first visit to $+1$. At $s=1$ the expression equals one, confirming eventual hitting with probability one.

= O
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Statistics}

= 1
{parent=O}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

For the sample <likelihood function> $L(\theta)=\prod_i f(X_i;\theta)$ and log-likelihood $\ell=\log L$, the expected <Fisher information> is
$$
\boxed{I_n(\theta)=\mathbb E_\theta[(\ell'(\theta))^2]=-\mathbb E_\theta[\ell''(\theta)].}
$$
The second equality uses the usual regularity assumptions permitting differentiation under the integral and a parameter-independent support. Independent identically distributed observations give $I_n(\theta)=nI_1(\theta)$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Under the standard consistency and regularity hypotheses for the <maximum-likelihood estimator>,
$$
\boxed{\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta)\xrightarrow{d}N\bigl(0,I_1(\theta)^{-1}\bigr).}
$$
Equivalently, for large samples $\widehat\theta$ is approximately $N(\theta,I_n(\theta)^{-1})$. In a standard-error estimate, the unknown parameter in the information may be replaced by the consistent estimate $\widehat\theta$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Put $Q=\sum_iX_i^2$. For $\theta>0$, the <log-likelihood> is $\ell(\theta)=\text{constant}-(n/2)\log\theta-Q/(2\theta)$. Its derivative is $-n/(2\theta)+Q/(2\theta^2)$, changing from positive to negative at $Q/n$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta=\frac1n\sum_iX_i^2.}
$$
The known mean is zero, so no centring at the sample mean is needed. Taking the expected negative second derivative gives $I_n(\theta)=n/(2\theta^2)$. The large-sample variance is consequently $2\theta^2/n$. With $z_{1-\alpha/2}$ the indicated standard-normal <quantile>, a <Wald confidence interval> is
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta\ \pm\ z_{1-\alpha/2}\widehat\theta\sqrt{2/n}.}
$$
The interval may be restricted to the parameter space $\theta>0$. The parameter is the variance itself, so the standard error is proportional to $\widehat\theta$, not to its square root.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Since the true mean is known to be zero, the <chi-squared distribution> gives $Q/\theta\sim\chi_n^2$. Let $q_\beta$ be the $\beta$ quantile of this law. The probability
$$
\mathbb P\left(q_{\alpha/2}\leq Q/\theta\leq q_{1-\alpha/2}\right)=1-\alpha
$$
rearranges, using positivity, to the exact <confidence interval>
$$
\boxed{\left[\frac{\sum_iX_i^2}{q_{1-\alpha/2}},\ \frac{\sum_iX_i^2}{q_{\alpha/2}}\right].}
$$
There are $n$ degrees of freedom, because no unknown mean was fitted.

= 2
{parent=O}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The <Neyman-Pearson lemma> concerns testing a simple null density $f_0$ against a simple alternative $f_1$. A test that rejects where $f_1/f_0>c$, accepts where the ratio is below $c$, and if necessary randomizes on the boundary to have null rejection probability $\alpha$, is a <most powerful test> among tests of size at most $\alpha$. Thus a likelihood-ratio threshold maximizes power at the specified simple alternative. The boundary randomization is relevant for discrete distributions.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

For $S=\sum_iX_i$, the ratio of the two sample <likelihood functions> is
$$
\frac{L(\mu_1)}{L(\mu_0)}
=e^{-n(\mu_1-\mu_0)}\left(\frac{\mu_1}{\mu_0}\right)^S.
$$
For $0<\mu_0<\mu_1$ it is strictly increasing in $S$. The <Neyman-Pearson lemma> therefore selects an upper tail:
$$
\boxed{C=\{\mathbf x:\textstyle\sum_i x_i\geq k\}.}
$$
Under the null, $S\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(n\mu_0)$, so choose $k$ with this upper-tail probability at most $\alpha$. If exact size $\alpha$ is required, randomize at the adjacent boundary value as needed. The rejection rule is the same for every $\mu_1>\mu_0$, giving a <uniformly most powerful test> for the one-sided alternative.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The total $S$ under $H_0$ has <Poisson distribution> with mean $12$, and the observed total is $16$. Its one-sided <p-value> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P_{H_0}(S\geq16)=\sum_{k=16}^\infty e^{-12}\frac{12^k}{k!}\approx0.1556.}
$$
The supplied $\phi_{12}(17)\approx0.1$ also shows that the observed count does not enter the approximate 10% upper-tail rejection region. Thus **there is insufficient evidence to reject $\mu=1$ at either the 10% or 5% level**. The question specifies no testing level, so the p-value gives the general conclusion: rejection is justified only at levels at least about $0.1556$.

The printed $\phi_1(16)\approx0$ concerns a Poisson variable with mean one, not the twelve-month total. It is therefore not the null tail for this data; substituting it would incorrectly treat the yearly count as one month's count.

= P
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= 1
{parent=P}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The <Gershgorin circle theorem> states that every <eigenvalue> of $A$ lies in at least one disc
$$
D_i=\left\{z\in\mathbb C:|z-a_{ii}|\leq\sum_{j\ne i}|a_{ij}|\right\}.
$$
For a nonzero complex <eigenvector> $v$, choose an index $i$ with $|v_i|=\max_j|v_j|>0$. The $i$th row of $Av=\lambda v$ gives
$$
|\lambda-a_{ii}|\,|v_i|\leq\sum_{j\ne i}|a_{ij}|\,|v_j|
\leq\left(\sum_{j\ne i}|a_{ij}|\right)|v_i|.
$$
Dividing by $|v_i|$ proves membership in $D_i$. This is valid for real matrices even when the eigenvalues are complex.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

For $A+\gamma B$ with $\gamma\geq0$, the three <Gershgorin discs> have positive real centres $a+\gamma$, $2c+2\gamma$, $6b+6\gamma$, and radii $b+c$, $a+b+\gamma$, $a+c+\gamma$. Centre minus radius is respectively
$$
a-b-c+\gamma>0,\qquad2c-a-b+\gamma>0,\qquad6b-a-c+5\gamma>0.
$$
Thus none of the discs contains zero. By the <Gershgorin circle theorem>, zero is not an <eigenvalue>, so $A+\gamma B$ is nonsingular for every $\gamma\geq0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\Gamma=\varnothing.}
$$

= 2
{parent=P}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Set $x=\cos\theta$ with $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$. The cosine addition identity gives
$$
\cos((n+1)\theta)+\cos((n-1)\theta)=2\cos\theta\cos(n\theta),
$$
hence $T_0=1$, $T_1=x$, and $T_{n+1}=2xT_n-T_{n-1}$. By <mathematical induction>, $T_n$ is a <polynomial> of degree $n$: the initial cases hold, and the leading term of $2xT_n$ has degree $n+1$ while the subtraction has lower degree. Its leading coefficient is $2^{n-1}$ for $n\geq1$.

The same change of variables gives the weighted <integral>
$$
\int_{-1}^1\frac{T_n(x)}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\,dx=\int_0^\pi\cos(n\theta)\,d\theta
=\boxed{\begin{cases}\pi,&n=0,\\0,&n\geq1.\end{cases}}
$$

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Since $T_2=2x^2-1$, take $x_1=-1/\sqrt2$, $x_2=1/\sqrt2$. Exactness for $T_0$ and $T_1$ requires $A+B=\pi$ and $(-A+B)/\sqrt2=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{x_{1,2}=\mp\frac1{\sqrt2},\qquad A=B=\frac\pi2.}
$$
For $T_2$, both node values and its weighted integral vanish. For $T_3=4x^3-3x$, oddness and symmetric nodes likewise give zero on both sides. Therefore $R(T_j)=0$ for $j=0,1,2,3$. These four <Chebyshev polynomials> have successive degrees zero through three and form a <basis> of the degree-at-most-three polynomial space. By <linearity>, the rule is exact on that whole space: this is two-node <Gauss–Chebyshev quadrature>.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

At either quadrature node, $2x_j^2=1$, so $u(x_j)=\arctan1=\pi/4$. Thus the <Gaussian quadrature> approximation is
$$
\boxed{\frac\pi2\frac\pi4+\frac\pi2\frac\pi4=\frac{\pi^2}{4}.}
$$
This is the requested quadrature value; the integrand is not a cubic polynomial, so the degree-three exactness established in part (b) does not make this the exact integral.
